                "text": " for these points.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Since $(m,n)$ lies on the graph and $m\\ne0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}n&=am^2\\\\a&=\\frac{n}{m^2}.\\end{align*}$$\nHence the graph has equation\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac{n}{m^2}x^2\\\\m^2y&=nx^2.\\end{align*}$$\nFor any positive integer $k$, let $x=km$ and $y=k^2n$. Both coordinates are integers, and\n$$\\begin{align*}m^2y&=m^2k^2n\\\\nx^2&=n(km)^2\\\\nx^2&=m^2k^2n.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $m^2y=nx^2$, so $(km,k^2n)$ lies on the graph. Since $m\\ne0$, different positive integers $k$ give different $x$-coordinates. Therefore there are infinitely many lattice points.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
